  "prediction": "Answer_9:\nThe winner got 3/4 of the votes.\nSo the loser got 1/4 of the votes.\nThe total number of students who voted is 80.\nSo the loser got 1/4 * 80 = 20 votes.\nThe answer is 20.\n\nAnswer_10:\nSandra ordered 4 times as many red roses as white carnations.\nShe also ordered 200 pink calla lilies, which were five times the number of white carnations.\nLet's say she ordered x white carnations.\nThen she ordered 4x red roses and 5x pink calla lilies.\nWe know that 5x = 200.\nSo x = 200 / 5 = 40.\nThen she ordered 4x = 4 * 40 = 160 red roses.\nThe answer is 160.\n\nAnswer_11:\nJan is 30 years old.\nTwo years ago, Mark was 5 years older than half Jan's age.\nTwo years ago, Jan was 30 - 2 = 28 years old.\nHalf Jan's age is 28 / 2 = 14.\nSo two years ago, Mark was 14 + 5 = 19 years old.\nNow Mark is 19 + 2 = 21 years old.\nJean is two years older than Mark.\nSo Jean is 21 + 2 = 23 years old.\nThe answer is 23.\n\nAnswer_12:\nThe well-rounded team runs each leg in 55 seconds.\nThe less well-rounded team runs the first leg in 60 seconds.\nEach subsequent runner is 3 seconds faster.\nSo the second runner runs in 60 - 3 = 57 seconds.\nThe third runner runs in 57 - 3 = 54 seconds.\nThe fourth runner runs in 54 - 3 = 51 seconds.\nThe total time for the well-rounded team is 55 * 4 = 220 seconds.\nThe total time for the less well-rounded team is 60 + 57 + 54 + 51 = 222 seconds.\nThe well-rounded team wins by 222 - 220 = 2 seconds.\nThe answer is 2.\n\nAnswer_13:\nJerry is rolling a six-sided die.\nThere are 6 possible outcomes.\n3 of them are greater than 3.\n2 of them are even numbers.\nThe probability of rolling a number greater than 3 is 3/6 = 1/2.\nThe probability of rolling two even numbers in a row is 1/2 * 1/2 = 1/4.\nThe difference is 1/2 - 1/4 = 1/4.\nTo express this as a percentage, multiply by 100.\n1/4 * 100 = 25.\nThe answer is 25.\n\nAnswer_14:\nJenna's mom got 20 apples.\nJenna picked half as many apples as her mom.\nSo Jenna picked 20 / 2 = 10 apples.\nThe answer is 10.\n\nAnswer_15:\nThe rats are kept in 3 cages in equal groups.\nEach rat is given 6 pieces of straw.\nSo each cage has 6 / 3 = 2 rats.\nThere are 10 cages of hamsters.\nEach hamster is given 5 pieces of straw.\nSo there are 10 * 5 = 50 hamsters.\nThere are 160 pieces of straw in total.\nThe rats get 2 * 6 = 12 pieces of straw per cage.\nSo there are 160 / 12 = 13.33 cages of rats.\nSince there are only 3 cages of rats, the number of rats per cage is 3.\nThe answer is 3.\n\nAnswer_16:\nMandy owes Benedict $100.\nThey agreed to have monthly interest of 2%.\nSo the interest for 3 months is 2% * 3 = 6%.\nThe interest is 6% of $100.\n6% of $100 is 0.06 * 100 = $6.\nSo Mandy should give Benedict $100 + $6 = $106.\nThe answer is 106.\n\nAnswer_17:\nThe trader buys bags of wheat at $20 each.\nHe sells them at $30 each.\nSo his profit per bag is $30 - $20 = $10.\nHe made a total profit of $400.\nSo he sold 400 / 10 = 40 bags.\nThe answer is 40.\n\nAnswer_18:\nThree friends spent $20.25 on 3 tickets to the fair.\nThey also spent $4.50 less on food than on the tickets.\nSo they spent $20.25 - $4.50 = $15.75 on food.\nThey also went on 2 different rides which costs $33 for each ride.\nSo they spent 2 * $33 = $66 on rides.\nIn total, they spent $20.25 + $15.75 + $66 = $102.\nThey agreed to split all the costs evenly.\nSo each of them paid $102 / 3 = $34.\nThe answer is 34.\n\nAnswer_19:\nJohn collects peaches for 3 hours.\nHe can collect 2 peaches a minute.\nSo he collects 2 * 60 = 120 peaches in an hour.\nIn 3 hours, he collects 120 * 3 = 360 peaches.\nThe answer is 360.\n\nAnswer_20:\nDucks need to eat 3.5 pounds of insects each week.\nThere is a flock of ten ducks.\nSo they need 3.5 * 10 = 35 pounds of insects per week.\nTo find out how many pounds of insects they need per day, divide by 7.\n35 / 7 = 5.\nThe answer is 5.\n\nAnswer_21:\nTom plants 10 trees a year.\nHe also chops down 2 trees a year.\nSo he has 10 - 2 = 8 trees per year.\nHe starts with 50 trees.\nAfter 10 years, he will have 50 + 8 * 10 = 150 trees.\n30% of the trees die.\nSo 30% of 150 is 0.3 * 150 = 45 trees.\nHe has 150 - 45 = 105 trees left.\nThe answer is 105.\n\nAnswer_22:\nThe neighbors placed 18 pink plastic flamingos out on Sue's front yard.\nOn Saturday morning, they took back one third of the flamingos.\nOne third of 18 is 18 / 3 = 6.\nSo they took back 6 flamingos.\nThey painted them white and put them back out.\nSo there are 6 white flamingos.\nOn Sunday morning, they added another 18 pink plastic flamingos.\nSo there are 18 + 6 = 24 pink flamingos.\nThere are 6 white flamingos.\nThere are 24 - 6 = 18 more pink flamingos than white flamingos.\nThe answer is 18.\n\nAnswer_23:\nVioletta needs crayons in 5 different colors.\nOne crayon costs $2.\nSo she needs 5 * $2 = $10.\nShe prepared $20 for this purchase.\nSo she will get $20 - $10 = $10 change.\nThe answer is 10.\n\nAnswer_24:\nA pen costs as much as a pencil and eraser combined.\nA pencil costs $1.20 and an eraser costs $0.30.\nSo a pen costs $1.20 + $0.30 = $1.50.\n8 pens will cost 8 * $1.50 = $12.\nThe answer is 12.\n\nAnswer_25:\n200 pounds of carrots are to be distributed to 40 restaurants.\nEach restaurant is to receive 2 pounds of carrots.\nSo 40 * 2 = 80 pounds of carrots will be used.\nThere will be 200 - 80 = 120 pounds of carrots left.\nThe answer is 120.\n\nAnswer_26:\nThere are 23786 inhabitants in the city of Soda.\n8417 men and 9092 women make up 8417 + 9092 = 17509 inhabitants.\nThe rest of the population is made up of children.\nSo there are 23786 - 17509 = 6277 children.\nThe answer is 6277.\n\nAnswer_27:\nElaine initially had 20 Pokemon cards.\nAfter a month, she collected three times that number.\nSo she collected 3 * 20 = 60 Pokemon cards.\nIn the second month, she collected 20 fewer cards than those she collected in the first month.\nSo she collected 60 - 20 = 40 Pokemon cards.\nIn the third month, she collected twice the combined number of Pokemon cards she collected in the first and second months.\nSo she collected 2 * (60 + 40) = 2 * 100 = 200 Pokemon cards.\nIn total, she collected 20 + 60 + 40 + 200 = 320 Pokemon cards.\nThe answer is 320.\n\nAnswer_28:\nMark edits an equal number of sentences each week for two different publishers.\nPublisher B pays Mark twice what Publisher A pays.\nPublisher A pays him 5 cents per sentence.\nSo Publisher B pays him 5 * 2 = 10 cents per sentence.\nMark edits a total number of 1000 sentences each week.\nPublisher A pays him 5 cents per sentence.\nSo Publisher A pays him 5 * 1000 = $50.\nPublisher B pays him 10 cents per sentence.\nSo Publisher B pays him 10 * 1000 = $100.\nIn total, Mark makes $50 + $100 = $150.\nThe answer is 150.\n\nAnswer_29:\nBaldur gets 5 pails of water every morning and 6 pails of water every afternoon.\nSo he gets 5 + 6 = 11 pails of water per day.\nEach pail contains 5 liters of water.\nSo he gets 11 * 5 = 55 liters of water per day.\nThe answer is 55.\n\nAnswer_30:\nJohn wins an award at work.\nThe award has a 1 time monetary reward of $5000.\nHe also gets a 5% raise in salary.\nHe makes $2000 a week, every week of the year.\nSo he makes 2000 * 52 = $104,000 per year.\nA 5% raise is 5% of $104,000.\n5% of $104,000 is 0.05 * 104,000 = $5200.\nSo his new salary is $104,000 + $5200 = $109,200.\nHe gets the award on the first day of the year.\nSo he gets the raise for 52 weeks.\nIn total, he makes $109,200 + $5000 = $114,200.\nThe answer is 114200.\n\nAnswer_31:\nJosie grows grapes on her 10-acre farm.\nEach acre produces 5 tons of grapes per year.\nSo her farm produces 10 * 5 = 50 tons of grapes per year.\nEach ton of grapes makes 2 barrels of wine.\nSo her farm produces 50 * 2 = 100 barrels of wine per year.\nThe answer is 100.\n\nAnswer_32:\nCarl ate a sandwich every day this week for lunch.\nHe used 2 slices of cheese on each sandwich.\nSo he used 2 * 7 = 14 slices of cheese on sandwiches.\nHe ate cheese and egg omelets for breakfast 3 days in the week.\nHe used one more slice per omelet than he did per sandwich.\nSo he used 3 * 3 = 9 slices of cheese on omelets.\nHe made a big dish of macaroni and cheese to last him several dinners for the week.\nHe used 8 slices of cheese in it.\nIn total, he used 14 + 9 + 8 = 31 slices of cheese.\nThe answer is 31.\n\nAnswer_33:\nJanet had 22 green pens and 10 yellow pens.\nThen she bought 6 bags of blue pens and 2 bags of red pens.\nThere were 9 pens in each bag of blue and 6 pens in each bag of red.\nSo she bought 6 * 9 = 54 blue pens and 2 * 6 = 12 red pens.\nIn total, she bought 54 + 12 = 66 pens.\nShe already had 22 + 10 = 32 pens.\nSo she now has 32 + 66 = 98 pens.\nThe answer is 98.\n\nAnswer_34:\nBrinley has scores of 89, 71, 92, 100 and 86 on the first five tests.\nShe needs to get an average of 93 on 6 tests.\nThe total score she needs is 93 * 6 = 558.\nThe total score she has so far is 89 + 71 + 92 + 100 + 86 = 338.\nShe needs to get 558 - 338 = 220 on the sixth test.\nThe answer is 220.\n\nAnswer_35:\nMicheal rode his bike at least 5 times a week and makes 25 kilometers each time.\nHe did that for four weeks.\nSo he rode 5 * 25 * 4 = 500 kilometers in the first four weeks.\nHe then rode the bike only 2 times a week, but for 60 kilometers each time.\nHe did that for 3 weeks.\nSo he rode 2 * 60 * 3 = 360 kilometers in the next three weeks.\nIn total, he rode 500 + 360 = 860 kilometers.\nThe answer is 860.\n\nAnswer_36:\nJoe's bag of Halloween candy has 25 chocolate bars and 80 candied apples.\nEach chocolate bar weighs twice as much as each candied apple.\nSo each chocolate bar weighs 2 * 40g = 80g.\nThe total weight of the chocolate bars is 25 * 80g = 2000g.\nThe total weight of the candied apples is 80 * 40g = 3200g.\nIn total, the bag of candy weighs 2000g + 3200g = 5200g.\nThe answer is 5200.\n\nAnswer_37:\nRose went to the store on Monday and bought 4 cakes.\nTuesday she went to a different store and bought three times that number of cakes.\nSo she bought 3 * 4 = 12 cakes.\nOn Wednesday she went to another store and bought 5 times the number of cakes she did on Tuesday.\nSo she bought 5 * 12 = 60 cakes.\nIn total, she bought 4 + 12 + 60 = 76 cakes.\nThe answer is 76.\n\nAnswer_38:\nTwenty dozen cups cost $1200 less than the total cost of half a dozen plates sold at $6000 each.\nTwenty dozen cups is 20 * 12 = 240 cups.\nHalf a dozen plates is 6 plates.\nThe total cost of half a dozen plates is 6 * $6000 = $36,000.\nTwenty dozen cups cost $36,000 - $1200 = $34,800.\nTo find the cost of each cup, divide by 240.\n$34,800 / 240 = $145.\nThe answer is 145.",
